{"explanation":"Rule: the reactions entry says that when you take a reaction, you can't take another one until the start of your next turn. An opportunity attack uses your reaction (opportunity-attacks entry). Fact: you used your reaction on the last turn of round 1, and in round 2 the enemy leaves your reach before your turn. Your next turn has not started yet, so your reaction has not come back. Conclusion: you cannot make the opportunity attack.

The conclusion turns on how long a reaction stays spent. The Ready entry says instead that 'you can take only one reaction per round.' Read that way, round 2 is a new round, you have not used a reaction in it, and you could make the attack. The two entries give opposite verdicts here.

The Ready entry presents its line as a reminder ('Remember that...'), which suggests it only summarises the reactions entry. That favours the 'until the start of your next turn' rule, which is the one I apply. But the text never says which wording controls, and it never defines 'round' in a way that settles the gap. The text therefore allows the other reading, and the conflict is flagged.","ambiguous":true,"answer":{"kind":"yes-or-no","verdict":"no"},"cites":["the-order-of-combat > reactions","making-an-attack > melee-attacks > opportunity-attacks","actions-in-combat > ready"]}

{"explanation":"The rule in equipment > net says that when you use an action, bonus action, or reaction to attack with a net, you can make only one attack, regardless of the number of attacks you can normally make. You can normally attack twice when you take the Attack action, which matches Extra Attack ('attack twice, instead of once, whenever you take the Attack action'). But if you attack with a net as part of that action, the action yields only one attack, so you cannot throw a second net with it.

Two-weapon fighting does not give another route to a second net. It needs light melee weapons, and the net is a 'Martial ranged weapon' with only the Thrown and Special properties. It is not a light melee weapon, so it cannot be used for the bonus-action attack.

The conclusion turns on the phrase 'you can make only one attack regardless of the number of attacks you can normally make.' The text allows no other reading that would permit two net attacks in one action. So you cannot throw two nets.","ambiguous":false,"answer":{"kind":"yes-or-no","verdict":"no"},"cites":["equipment > net","class-features > extra-attack","actions-in-combat > attack","making-an-attack > melee-attacks > two-weapon-fighting"]}
